# closing
## The back bedroom, at midnight
$ do witness(0)
It is the last hour before the morning. The house has gone quiet in the way of houses which have a guest in them, with every board carefully not creaking. You go up. There is a lamp on the landing, and the door is a little open.
He is standing. It is the first time that you have seen him stand. He has put on somebody's old overcoat, which comes to the middle of his shins, and has put the paper in a neat square on the bed, and is looking at the shutters with his hand on the latch, and has not opened it.
?if b5.lisle == "court"
  "I have written to Lady Marchmont," he says, without turning. "I said that I would see her. I said that I would not be fetched."
?elif b5.lisle == "hidden"
  "I am a prisoner, I find," he says, without turning, "and I find that I do not at all mind. I shall want to be a good deal less of one by the spring."
?else
  "It is a very good key," he says, without turning, "and I have not yet found what it is for."
?end
?if b5.vane == "pleaded"
  "I am told that Vane is not to be shut up," he says. "I am told that you spoke. I do not know what to say. I have not got the words for it." He turns round. His face is wet.
?elif b5.vane == "judged"
  "I am told that they have given him Hampstead, and a book," he says. "It is better than I thought. It is not what he deserved." He turns round. "I should like to go to him."
?else
  "I am told that nothing has been decided about Vane," he says, "and I know very well what that means. It means that somebody has decided, and has not liked to say." He turns round. His eyes are very bright.
?end
"There is a thing," says Lisle, "that I have not told you. I did not like to, while I had a cup in my hand." He sits down on the edge of the bed. "I was not shut up for a madman. I was shut up for a reader."
:the_telling
He tells it very plainly, as a man reads out an inventory. In the year 1587, the twelfth of the Court of Lamps, walking in the ruin of the Roman wall by Bishopsgate, found a chamber. It had a door, and the door had been made of cedar, and over the door a mark that had not been cut by any mason of any nation that he knew. He opened it. There was a lamp inside, a plain thing of black clay, that had been burning for a very long time. It was not like the lamps of the Court. It was the thing that they had been made to look like.
"I read it," says Lisle. "It is a kind of writing that is not writing, and it was not meant for a man. It was meant, I think, for the first. There was a first, before the six, who dug the pit and cut the cedar and set the chain. I never saw the face. I only saw what it left. There is a thing in that room that holds a very great deal still. And when I had read it, something heard me." He has put his hands between his knees. "I was not mad. I was *attended*. The Eleven saw it, and were afraid, and did the sensible thing."
He looks up.
"I should like to go back," he says. "I should like to read it again, now that I am not so young. I think that it is breaking. I think that it has been breaking for some years, and that it is a very bad thing indeed that nobody has been to look."
?if has_gift("old_blood")
  The old blood in you has gone very quiet and very cold. It is not fear. It is something older and more patient. It is a thing that knows that it has been asked.
?end
:the_first
* Say that you will go with him, the night after next, with whoever he likes [once]
  $ b5.first = "agreed"
  $ humanity += 1
  $ standing += 1
  "Thank you," says Lisle. It is such a small ordinary phrase, in such a very old mouth, that you cannot answer it. He puts out his hand, and you take it, and it is dry and light and quite steady.
* Say that the family will go first, and see the door, and bring him word [once]
  $ b5.first = "scouted"
  $ cunning_brutal += 1
  $ secrecy += 1
  "Very wise," says Lisle. "It is what I should have done at your age." He is not offended. He is relieved, a little, and ashamed to be. "You will find that the wall has been built on. They have put a brewery on it."
* Say that he is not to go until he has his strength, and that it will be a week [once]
  $ b5.first = "waited"
  $ cunning_brutal += 2
  $ humanity -= 1
  "That," says Lisle, after a pause, "is the sensible answer. It is what the Eleven said. It is, I find, rather a difficult thing to hear from the one who woke me." He smiles, very faintly. "I shall obey. I shall not like it."
-> dawn

# dawn
## Before the dark ends
The window, when he opens it at last, gives on a yard, and a pump, and a very small and ordinary plane tree, and over it the sky going from black to the color of a pigeon. He stands with his hands on the sill and says nothing at all. Then he says that it is a very ugly sky, and that he has been wanting to see it for three hundred years.
?if b5.meeting == "alone"
  Cassian is on the landing outside, in the dark, with his hat in his hand. He has not been invited. He has been, apparently, waiting for it to be time.
?elif b5.meeting == "apart"
  There is a sound from the attic, above, of somebody pacing, in a regular and a very patient way.
?end
?if b5.vane == "pleaded" or b5.vane == "judged"
  A note has come, on the hall table, in a thin careful hand, from Hampstead. It is four lines long and has a lamp drawn at the foot of it.
?end
?if b5.first == "waited"
  Something, you notice, has not been said. It is in the house like a draught. It is the thing that Cassian did not say, in the attic, with his book the right way up.
?end
Somewhere across the square a shutter is let down, with great discretion, and a milk cart begins its slow round. It is the seventh of October. The house is awake and the sleeper is on his feet.
